    answer: 'C',
    explanation: 'We want integers divisible by exactly 2 of {4, 6, 10}. LCMs: lcm(4,6)=12, lcm(4,10)=20, lcm(6,10)=30, lcm(4,6,10)=60. Count of integers divisible by exactly two: |div by 4 and 6 but not 10| + |div by 4 and 10 but not 6| + |div by 6 and 10 but not 4|. Div by 12 but not 10: div by 12 but not lcm(12,10)=60. Below 200: ⌊199/12⌋=16, ⌊199/60⌋=3 → 16−3=13. Div by 20 but not 6: div by 20 but not lcm(20,6)=60. Below 200: ⌊199/20⌋=9, ⌊199/60⌋=3 → 9−3=6. Div by 30 but not 4: div by 30 but not lcm(30,4)=60. Below 200: ⌊199/30⌋=6, ⌊199/60⌋=3 → 6−3=3. But we have already excluded those divisible by all three (lcm=60) which are divisible by all three, not exactly two. Wait — the formulas above already exclude "all three" by subtracting out multiples of 60. Check: div by 12 not 10: includes div by 12, excludes div by 60 → these are div by 4 and 6 but not necessarily not div by 10 unless 60 is the only overlap. Actually div by 12 and not 10 means div by 4&6 but any number div by 10&12 must be div by lcm(10,12)=60. So 13 is correct for the first group. Total = 13+6+3 = 22. But let\'s verify with a smaller sanity check. Multiples of 60 below 200: 60,120,180 = 3 numbers (div by all three). 16+9+6 divisors respectively, minus 3 each twice-counted for all-three, total for at-least-two... Alternatively: exact answer is 13+6+3=22. Answer: E (22).',
