    answer: 1,
    explanation: '"Exhausted from years of litigation" is a participial phrase that must describe the subject of the main clause. The plaintiffs are what can be exhausted — not a settlement offer. (B) correctly places "the plaintiffs" as the subject after the comma. (A) dangling: the settlement offer cannot be exhausted. (C) inserts "it was" which is an expletive construction and still has a dangling modifier issue. (D) eliminates the modifier entirely and introduces "three times less" which is logically problematic. (E) moves "exhausted" to a parenthetical mid-sentence — grammatically acceptable but wordy and restructured beyond the correction needed. GMAT prefers the most concise correction: (B). Trap: (E) fixes the logic but is unnecessarily verbose. Target: 1:20',
