    explanation: 'The sentence describes a continuous action (working on municipal projects) that will be completed at a future reference point (retirement next year). Future perfect progressive "will have been working" is the correct tense — it emphasizes ongoing duration up to a future endpoint. (A) is correct. (B) "will be working" (future progressive) does not convey the duration/completion nuance of "for over three decades." (C) "has been working" is present perfect progressive, but "before she will retire" is redundant and the tense sequence is off. (D) uses simple present "is working" which does not project to the future. (E) uses past perfect "had been working" with "next year" — a contradiction (past perfect + future reference). Trap: (B) sounds natural but omits the completion aspect that "will have been" conveys. Target: 1:30',
