    explanation: 'Two issues: (1) "both ... as well as" is incorrect — the correct correlative is "both ... and." (2) "was attributed to" is passive and slightly wordy. (B) fixes the idiom with "both ... and" and uses the modal "could be attributed to" appropriately. (A) uses "both ... as well as" — wrong correlative. (C) fixes "as well as" but drops "both" and uses "attributable to" — grammatically acceptable but loses the explicit "both ... and" parallelism. (D) uses "and also to" — the "also" is redundant with the implied contrast already in the sentence. (E) restructures the sentence but keeps "both ... as well as" error. Trap: "as well as" sounds like a valid correlative but "both ... and" is the required pairing. Target: 1:20',
