    answer: 1,
    explanation: 'The prediction is that going from 15g to 30g (a 15g increase) would reduce CHD by approximately 20%. (B) provides the quantitative mechanism: each 10g increase yields approximately 10% risk reduction. A 15g increase would therefore produce approximately 15% reduction — close to 20%, and the dose-response relationship supports the directional claim. This is the most rigorous evidence directly validating the mathematical prediction. (A) is vague — fiber is part of a healthy diet, but this does not validate the specific 20% claim. (C) introduces confounders (exercise, smoking) that actually raise the question of whether fiber causes the benefit or just correlates with a healthier lifestyle — this slightly weakens the causal argument. (D) addresses implementation feasibility, not the validity of the 20% claim. (E) is about the magnitude of impact if true — it does not validate whether it is true. Trap: (C) sounds like supporting evidence (populations with high fiber have better heart health) but actually introduces a confound. Target: 2:00',
