    type: 'CR',
    section: 'Verbal',
    difficulty: 'hard',
    topic: 'Bold-Face',
    question: 'Some urban planners argue that constructing high-speed rail corridors between major cities will reduce automobile traffic and improve air quality. Transportation economists have shown that [BOLD 1: every major high-speed rail project completed in Europe over the past 30 years reduced intercity car travel along the affected corridors by at least 18%.] However, critics note that [BOLD 2: the United States has a fundamentally different urban settlement pattern from Europe, with far lower population densities and greater distances between cities.] Which of the following best describes the roles of the two bolded statements?',
