    explanation: 'The data shows that from Q8–Q12 (spend $55K–$75K), acquisitions grew only from 430 to 450 — a gain of 20 customers over a $20K spend increase, or 1 customer per $1K at the margin. To go from 450 to 500 (50 more customers) at this marginal rate would require roughly $50K more spend (to $125K), far above $80K. (B) captures this correctly.\n\n(A) Assumes a linear trend that the data contradicts; also "definitely" is too strong for an extrapolation.\n(B) Correct: diminishing returns imply high marginal cost for the next 50 acquisitions.\n(C) Too strong — "not achievable regardless of budget" is an overreach; the data suggests diminishing returns, not a hard cap.\n(D) Reducing spend in the face of diminishing returns at high spend makes no logical connection.\n(E) Inferences from trends are a core purpose of scatter plot analysis.\n\nTrap: (A) is the classic linear-extrapolation trap that ignores the clearly visible flattening at high spend. Target: 2:30',
